{"chunk_id": "23da3d5a2404", "text": "This section centers on the humbling power of Time and the shifting nature of prosperity. The fallen Asura king Vali explains to Indra that neither gods nor heroes truly act independently; Time (identified with Brahman or cosmic eternity) governs creation, destruction, and the rise and fall of power. Even mighty rulers\u2014countless Indras before the present one\u2014are eventually swept away. After this discourse, the goddess Sree (Lakshmi), embodiment of prosperity, visibly departs from Vali and appears before Indra. She explains that she dwells where truth, generosity, discipline, sacrifice, and virtue prevail, and that Vali lost her through arrogance and impiety. At Indra\u2019s request, she divides herself into four portions placed in earth, water, fire, and the virtuous among humans. The episode emphasizes impermanence, moral conduct as the basis of fortune, and Time\u2019s ultimate sovereignty.\nVali explains Time\u2019s supremacy over gods and power; the goddess Sree leaves him for Indra and is divided among earth, water, fire, and the virtuous.\nThemes: supremacy of Time, impermanence of power, divine prosperity, virtue and decline, cosmic order", "vector": [0.02486533112823963, -0.020044362172484398, -0.007020959164947271, -0.003995971288532019, -0.0240505188703537, 0.013023403473198414, -0.020641891285777092, -0.02094065584242344, -0.016703635454177856, -0.02783939242362976, 0.025204835459589958, 0.0020030783489346504, 0.025734463706612587, 0.008555520325899124, -0.016554253175854683, -0.004844733513891697, 0.043510932475328445, -0.013749944046139717, 0.01894436776638031, -0.015399937517940998, -0.00016869144747033715, 0.004488253500312567, -0.0007817097939550877, 0.010456747375428677, -0.010558598674833775, 0.0003305927966721356, 0.02037028782069683, -0.02232583425939083, 0.027173962444067, -0.007374044042080641, -0.0018486035987734795, 0.0018655788153409958, -0.007577746640890837, -0.008406138978898525, -0.01201167